\f5\i \cf4 \cb3 \uc0\u964 
\f0\i0\fs28  generates three quarter-turn planes.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 Explore which transformations preserve the table.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf4 \cb3 Then ask students to identify four conserved quantities under appropriate continuous symmetries.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf4 \cb3 Finally, challenge them to construct an effective relativistic metric and recover the invariant mass relation.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf4 \cb3 That would turn a conventional lecture into an actual mathematical research exercise.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf4 \cb3 And students would discover something important along the way:\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf4 \cb3 The geometry of the space in which we represent a physical system need not be identical to the geometry governing its physically observable intervals.
\f0\b0 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 That's an idea worth understanding, whether or not your proposed timespace model ultimately succeeds.\cf0 \cb1 \
\cf4 \cb3 The educational payoff is real even before any new physics is established.\cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

